  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nEvident on satellite water vapor imagery, an upper-level low \ncurrently sits centered on the North Dakota-Montana-Saskatchewan\nborder, with longwave troughing extending well south into the \nfour corners region. To the East, the mid-atlantic is \nexperiencing appreciably-strong upper-level ridging. Due to our \nlocation wedged between these synoptic regimes, modestly strong \nsouthwesterly winds are advecting an unseasonably warm air mass \nacross the CWA, with temperatures approaching record highs. \nWhile the air mass is currently relatively dry, a moisture \ntongue to our west looks to drift into the area, bringing our \nPWAT values up near 1.1\", which is about 3 standard deviations \nabove normal in the Sunday morning hours.\n\nBehind this moist air mass, the longwave troughing regime looks\nto eject eastward, bringing with it an elongated cold front and\nseasonably cool temperatures. This cold front, along with the \nright entrance region of the upper-level jet streak and broad \nheight falls, will serve as ample forcing for a period of rain \nshowers during the daytime hours Sunday. While mid-level lapse \nrates are poor, dynamic forcing and brief pockets of elevated \ninstability could combine for a few rumbles of thunder, \nparticularly in the southwest corner of the CWA. Following the \npassage of the cold front, blustery northwest winds will \nenvelope the area.\n\nIn the wake of this front, a substantially colder and drier air\nmass will build over the CWA on Monday, with daytime highs in \nthe mid 40s and nighttime lows in the low 20s to high teens. \nWhile prevailing North/Northeast surface flow supports a quality\nfetch off of Lake Michigan, a strong inversion ~1km AGL should \nlimit lake effect precip to some flurries or light snow showers.\n\nFurther into the week, modeled predictability takes a notable \ndownturn. What's known is that a cutoff upper-level low in the \nmidwest will interact with a digging trough on the east coast. \nWhat's unknown is how exactly these features interact. Recent \nmodels tend to form a surface low/inverted trough feature over \nMichigan. The deterministic euro and most GEFS ensemble members \nkeep the surface low moisture starved until it passes well East \nof our CWA. The deterministic GFS and the AIFS-suite (ensemble \nand deterministic) wrap a broad TROWAL over our CWA, with \npotential for mixed precip.\n\nAs there are major questions on evolution of the pattern, \ndegree of moisture return, and coolness of surface temperatures,\nconfidence remains low on significant winter precip during the \nThanksgiving timeframe. Holiday travelers should remain weather \naware, particularly if heading east of IL.\n\nWefer/KJB",
